\label{rmk: recursive formula norm ord}
The normal ordering of $n>2$ linear field polynomials $L_1,\ldots,L_n\in\mathcal{P}_{\textnormal{lin}}$ can be inductively proven to satisfy the formula
\begin{align*}
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, L_n \, L_{n-1} \cdots L_1 \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    \,=\,
    L_n \, \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, L_{n-1} \cdots L_1 \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    \,+\,
    \sum_{i=1}^{n-1}
    (-1)^i\,
    \wick{\c L_n \, \c L_{n-i}}\,
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, L_{n-1} \cdots \widehat{L}_{n-i} \cdots L_1 \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,\,,
\end{align*}
where the hat ($\,\widehat{\phantom{m}}\;\!$) indicates the factor is not in the product.
\hfill$\diamond$

\label{rmk: norm ord surjective}
The map $\, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,\cdot\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,\colon \mathcal{P}\longrightarrow\mathcal{P}$ is surjective.
To see this, note that, for a field polynomial~$P$ of degree~$d$, the field polynomial $\, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,P\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,-P$ is of degree at most $d-2$.
That is, the map $\, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,\cdot\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,$ is upper triangular with $1$s on the diagonal.
\hfill$\diamond$

\label{rmk: norm ord factors through null fields}
Let $L_1,\ldots,L_n\in\mathcal{P}_{\textnormal{lin}}$ be linear field polynomials.
If $L_i$ is null for some $i\in\{1,\ldots,n\}$, then $\, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,L_1 \cdots L_n\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,$ is a null field polynomial too.
To prove this, one first needs to expand the expression of the correlation function
\begin{align*}
    \Big\langle 
    \textnormal{ev}^{\Omega^{\delta}}_{\mathbf{z}}
    \big( \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,L_1 \cdots L_n\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, \big)\,
    \xi(\mathbf{z}_1)\,\theta(\mathbf{w}_1)
    \cdots
    \xi(\mathbf{z}_m)\,\theta(\mathbf{w}_m)
     \Big\rangle_{\Omega^{\delta}}^{\textnormal{fDGFF}}
\end{align*}
by means of Wick's formula ---Remark~\ref{rmk: 2n pt function fDGFF}--- and the definition of normal ordering.
The proof eventually boils down to the observation that we have
\begin{align*}
    \Big\langle 
    \textnormal{ev}^{\Omega^{\delta}}_{\mathbf{z}}
    \big( \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,L_1L_2\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,\big)\, \Big\rangle_{\Omega^{\delta}}^{\textnormal{fDGFF}}
    \,=\,0
\end{align*}
whenever $L_1$ or $L_2$ are null linear fields---recall the characterisation of null linear fields in Corollary~\ref{coro: charac null linear} and the Example~\ref{ex: norm ord of two fields}.
We refer the reader to \cite[Lemma~6.2]{ABK-DGFF_local_fields} for a detailed proof of this statement in the bosonic setting.
\hfill$\diamond$

