We write down the details only for the case $\overline n = \overline m = 0$;
the proof in the general case follows similar arguments.
We proceed by induction on $n+m$.
The linear base cases
\begin{align*}
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \circ\,\circ$}}\, \boldsymbol{\chi}_{-k} \mathbf{1} \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \circ\,\circ$}}\,
    \,=\,
    \boldsymbol{\chi}_{-k} \mathbf{1}\,,
    \mspace{40mu}
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \circ\,\circ$}}\, \boldsymbol{\eta}_{-k} \mathbf{1} \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \circ\,\circ$}}\,
    \,=\,
    \boldsymbol{\eta}_{-k} \mathbf{1}\,,
    \mspace{40mu}
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \circ\,\circ$}}\, \boldsymbol{\upxi} \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \circ\,\circ$}}\,
    \,=\,
    \boldsymbol{\upxi}\,,
    \mspace{20mu}
    \text{ and }
    \mspace{20mu}
