    \,=\,
    \boldsymbol{\uptheta}
\end{align*}
are straightforward to check since normal ordering $\, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,\cdots\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,$ is the identity map when restricted to linear field polynomials.
The value $\mathbf{G}_{{\mathbb Z}^2}(0)=0$ implies that we also have
\begin{align*}
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \circ\,\circ$}}\, \boldsymbol{\uptheta}\otimes\boldsymbol{\upxi} \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \circ\,\circ$}}\,
    \,=\,
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, \boldsymbol\uptheta(0)\boldsymbol\upxi(0)\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, + \textnormal{\small Null}
    \,=\,
    \boldsymbol\uptheta(0)\boldsymbol\upxi(0) + \,\textnormal{\small Null}
    \,=\,
    \boldsymbol{\upomega}\,.
\end{align*}
For the rest of the proof we focus only on the last case ---that is, the $\, \rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \circ\,\circ$}}\,T\otimes\boldsymbol{\uptheta}\otimes\boldsymbol{\upxi}\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \circ\,\circ$}}\,$ case---, the other three can be handled in a similar manner.
By the induction hypothesis, we have
\begin{align*}
    \boldsymbol{\chi}_{-k_n}\cdots\boldsymbol{\chi}_{-k_1}
    \boldsymbol{\eta}_{-\ell_m}\cdots\boldsymbol{\eta}_{-{\ell}_1}
    \boldsymbol{\upomega}
    =
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, F \,\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    + \textnormal{\small Null}
\end{align*}
with 
$F \, \coloneqq \,
{X}_{-k_n} \cdots {X}_{-k_1}
{H}_{-\ell_m} \cdots {H}_{-\ell_1}
{H}_{0} \, {X}_{0}$\,,
where ${X}_{k}$ and ${H}_{\ell}$ are the field-polynomial representatives of the linear fields in Examples~\ref{ex: fermion linear rep} and \ref{ex: rep of linear basis}.
In what follows, we also use the notation
\begin{align*}
    F_0^{\hat {H}} &\; \coloneqq \,
    {X}_{-k_n} \cdots {X}_{-k_1}
    {H}_{-\ell_m} \cdots {H}_{-\ell_1}
    {X}_{0}\,,










    \mspace{20mu}
    \text{ and }
    \\
    F_j^{\hat {H}} &\; \coloneqq \,
    {X}_{-k_n} \cdots  {X}_{-k_1}
    {H}_{-\ell_m} \cdots \widehat{{H}}_{-\ell_j} \cdots {H}_{-\ell_1}
    {H}_{0} \, {X}_{0}\,,
\end{align*}
where the hat ($\,\widehat{\phantom{m}}\;\!$) indicates the factor is not in the product.
We prove the inductive step for the induction in $n$---the induction in  $m$ can be handled almost identically.
Then, on the one hand, using the recursive formula in by Remark~\ref{rmk: recursive formula norm ord}, we have
\begin{align}
\label{eq: one hand}
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    {X}_{-k_{n+1}} F \,
    \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    \;=\; 
    {X}_{-k_{n+1}} \,
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, F \, \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    & \,
    - \;
    \sum_{i=0}^{m-1} (-1)^{n+i} \wick{ \c {X}_{-k_{n+1}} \c {H}_{-k_{m-i}} } \,
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,F^{\hat {H}}_{m-i}\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    \\
    \nonumber
    & \,
    - \;
    (-1)^{n+m}\,
    \wick{ \c {X}_{-k_{n+1}} \c {H}_{ 0 } } \,
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,F^{\hat {H}}_{0}\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,\,,
\end{align}
where we have already removed the terms with vanishing Wick contractions of the type $\wick{\c X \c X}$.
On the other hand, by the definition of the current modes ---Proposition~\ref{prop: current modes}--- and using the discrete Stokes' formula ---Section~\ref{subsubsec: discrete integration}---, we have
\begin{align*}
    \boldsymbol{\chi}_{-k_{n+1}} \big( \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,F \,\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, + \textnormal{\small Null}\big)
    \;=\; & \,
    {X}_{-k_{n+1}} \,
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, F \, \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    \;+\;
    \frac{1}{2\pi}
    \sum_{\mathbf{u}_\diamond}\mathbf{u}_\diamond^{[-k_{n+1}]} \, 
    \mathrlap{\hspace{1pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.8}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\partial}}}}}\ensuremath{\partial}
_\sharp\partial^\bullet_\sharp\boldsymbol\upxi(\mathbf{u}_\diamond) \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, F \, \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    + \textnormal{\small Null}
    \,,
\end{align*}
where the sum runs over a big enough neighbourhood of the origin.
Using the recursive formula in Remark~\ref{rmk: recursive formula norm ord} again, we can massage the last expression into
\begin{align}
\label{eq: other hand}
    \boldsymbol{\chi}_{-k_{n+1}} \big( \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,F \,\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, + \textnormal{\small Null}\big)
    \;=\; & \,
    {X}_{-k_{n+1}} \,
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, F \, \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    \; + \;
    \frac{1}{2\pi}
    \sum_{\mathbf{u}_\diamond}\mathbf{u}_\diamond^{[-k_{n+1}]}
    \mspace{-40mu}
    \overbrace{
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{1pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.8}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\partial}}}}}\ensuremath{\partial}
_\sharp\partial^\bullet_\sharp\boldsymbol\upxi(\mathbf{u}_\diamond) \phantom{\bigg\vert}\! F \, \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,}^{\text{null by Remark~\ref{rmk: factorisation laplacian} and Remark~\ref{rmk: norm ord factors through null fields}}}
    \\
    \nonumber
    &
    \mspace{30mu}
    \;+\;
    \frac{1}{2\pi}
    \sum_{\mathbf{u}_\diamond}\mathbf{u}_\diamond^{[-k_{n+1}]} \,
    \Bigg(
    \sum_{i=0}^{m-1} (-1)^{m+i}\,
    \wick{  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{1pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.8}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\partial}}}}}\ensuremath{\partial}
_\sharp\partial^\bullet_\sharp\c \boldsymbol\upxi(\mathbf{u}_\diamond) \c {H}_{-k_{m-i}} } \,
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,F^{\hat {H}}_{m-i}\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    \\
    \nonumber
    &
    \mspace{220mu}
    + \;
    (-1)^{n+m}\,
    \wick{  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{1pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.8}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\partial}}}}}\ensuremath{\partial}
_\sharp\partial^\bullet_\sharp\c \boldsymbol\upxi(\mathbf{u}_\diamond) \c {H}_{ 0 } } \,
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,F^{\hat {H}}_{0}\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    \Bigg)\,.
\end{align}
Comparing~\eqref{eq: one hand} and \eqref{eq: other hand} we see that
if we have
\begin{align}
\label{eq: key ingredient}
    \frac{1}{2\pi}
    \sum_{\mathbf{u}_\diamond}\mathbf{u}_\diamond^{[-k]} \,
    \wick{  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{1pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.8}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\partial}}}}}\ensuremath{\partial}
_\sharp\partial^\bullet_\sharp\c \boldsymbol\upxi(\mathbf{u}_\diamond) \c {H}_{-\ell} }
    \,+\,
    \wick{ \c {X}_{-k} \c {H}_{-\ell } }
    \,=\, 0
\end{align}
for all $k>0$ and $\ell\geq 0$, we get the desired
\begin{align*}
    \boldsymbol{\chi}_{-k_{n+1}} \big( \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,F \,\,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\, + \textnormal{\small Null}\big)
    \, = \,
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}
        {\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    {X}_{-k_{n+1}} F \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \bullet\,\bullet$}}\,
    \,+\, \textnormal{\small Null}\,.
\end{align*}
Using the expression of the linear representatives ---Example~\ref{ex: rep of linear basis}--- and using Stokes' formula again, \eqref{eq: key ingredient} is equivalent to
\begin{align*}
    0 \; = \; & \,
    \frac{1}{2\pi}
    \sum_{\mathbf{u}_\diamond}\mathbf{u}_\diamond^{[-k]} \,
    \wick{  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{1pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.8}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\partial}}}}}\ensuremath{\partial}
_\sharp\partial^\bullet_\sharp\c \boldsymbol\upxi(\mathbf{u}_\diamond) \c {H}_{-\ell} }
    \,+\,
    \frac{1}{2\pi}
    \sum_{\mathbf{u}_\textnormal{m}}

    \mathrlap{\hspace{1pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.8}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\partial}}}}}\ensuremath{\partial}
_\sharp \mathbf{u}_\textnormal{m}^{[-k]}
    \wick{ \partial^\bullet_\sharp \c \boldsymbol\upxi (\mathbf{u}_\textnormal{m}) \c {H}_{-\ell } }
    \\
    = \; & \,
    \frac{1}{2\pi\mathbbm{i}}
    \sqint_\gamma
    \mathbf{u}_\diamond^{[-k]}
    \wick{ \partial^\bullet_\sharp \c \boldsymbol\upxi (\mathbf{u}_\textnormal{m}) \c {H}_{-\ell } }\,
    \textnormal{d}^{\sharp} \mathbf{u}\,,
\end{align*}
which is indeed true by virtue of Lemma~\ref{lemma: technical}.

\label{lemma: univ prop Fock}
Let $V$ be a representation\footnote{We use the module notation for simplicity.} of $\textnormal{\textbf{SF}} \oplus \overline{\textnormal{\textbf{SF}}}$, and let $v\in V\setminus \{0\}$  be a non-zero vector satisfying
\begin{align*}
    \mspace{130mu}
    {\upchi}_{k}v
    \,=\,
    {\upeta}_{k}v
    \,=\,

    \mathrlap{\hspace{-0.6pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\upchi}}}}}\ensuremath{\upchi}
_{k}v
    \,=\,

    \mathrlap{\hspace{-0.6pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.97}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\upeta}}}}}\ensuremath{\upeta}
_{k}v
    \,=\,
    0 & \phantom{\Big\vert}
    \mspace{50mu}
    \text{ for } \mspace{5mu} k>0\,,
    \\
    \big( {\upeta}_{0} -  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{-0.6pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.97}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\upeta}}}}}\ensuremath{\upeta}
_{0} \big) v \,=\, 0\,,
    \mspace{50mu}
    \big( {\upchi}_{0} -  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{-0.6pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\upchi}}}}}\ensuremath{\upchi}
_{0} \big) v \, &\,=\, 0\,,\phantom{\Big\vert}
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
    {\upchi}_{0} {\upeta}_{0} v \,\neq\, 0 \,.
\end{align*}
Then there exists a unique $(\textnormal{\textbf{SF}} \oplus \overline{\textnormal{\textbf{SF}}})$-homomorphism $\mathscr{F}{\!\!\textnormal{\footnotesize\itshape ock}}\rightarrow V$ determined by 
\begin{align*}
    \boldsymbol{\omega} \longmapsto v\,.
\end{align*}
This map is injective and constitutes an isomorphism between $\mathscr{F}{\!\!\textnormal{\footnotesize\itshape ock}}$ and $(\mathcal{SF} \otimes  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{1.5pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}}}}}\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}
)v$ as representations of $\textnormal{\textbf{SF}} \oplus \overline{\textnormal{\textbf{SF}}}$.

The existence of such a map follows from the universal property of the quotient~$\mathscr{F}{\!\!\textnormal{\footnotesize\itshape ock}}$.
Uniqueness follows from the fact that the values of the map on the basis vectors are fixed by $\boldsymbol{\omega} \mapsto v$ and the homomorphism property.
Let $\phi\colon\mathscr{F}{\!\!\textnormal{\footnotesize\itshape ock}}\rightarrow V$ denote such a unique homomorphism.
By construction, the map $\phi$ maps $\mathscr{F}{\!\!\textnormal{\footnotesize\itshape ock}}$ surjectively onto the submodule $(\mathcal{SF} \otimes  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{1.5pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}}}}}\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}
)v$.
As for injectivity, since it is a non-zero map, its kernel must be a proper submodule of $\mathscr{F}{\!\!\textnormal{\footnotesize\itshape ock}}$.
Recall from Remark~\ref{rmk: subrepresentations} that the only three proper submodules of $\mathscr{F}{\!\!\textnormal{\footnotesize\itshape ock}}$
are $(\mathcal{SF} \otimes  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{1.5pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}}}}}\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}
)\boldsymbol{\xi}$, $(\mathcal{SF} \otimes  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{1.5pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}}}}}\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}
)\boldsymbol{\theta}$ and $(\mathcal{SF} \otimes  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{1.5pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}}}}}\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}
)\boldsymbol{\mathbbm{1}}$; the third one, in turn, is a proper submodule of each of the first two.
However, since we assumed
\begin{align*}
    \phi ( \boldsymbol{\mathbbm{1}} )
    \,=\,
    \phi ( {\upchi}_{0} {\upeta}_{0} \boldsymbol{\omega} )
    \,=\,
    {\upchi}_{0} {\upeta}_{0} \phi ( \boldsymbol{\omega} )
    \,=\,
    {\upchi}_{0} {\upeta}_{0} v
    \,\neq\,
    0\,,
\end{align*}
we must have $\ker \phi = \{0\}$, i.e.~$\phi$ is injective.
We conclude that $\phi$ is an isomorphism between $\mathscr{F}{\!\!\textnormal{\footnotesize\itshape ock}}$ and $(\mathcal{SF} \otimes  
    \mathrlap{\hspace{1.5pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}}}}}\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}
)v$.

The local field~$\boldsymbol{\upomega}$ satisfies
\begin{align*}
    \boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}\boldsymbol{\upomega}
    \,=\,
    \boldsymbol{\eta}_{k}\boldsymbol{\upomega}
    \,=\,

    \mathrlap{\hspace{0pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\chi}}}}}}\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\chi}}
_{k}\boldsymbol{\upomega}
    \,=\,

    \mathrlap{\hspace{0pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\eta}}}}}}\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\eta}}
_{k}\boldsymbol{\upomega}
    \,=\,
    0
\end{align*}
for all $k\in{\mathbb Z}_{>0}$ and
\begin{align*}
    \big(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{0}- 
    \mathrlap{\hspace{0pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\chi}}}}}}\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\chi}}
_{0}\big)
    \boldsymbol{\upomega}
    \,=\,
    0
    \,=\,
    \big(\boldsymbol{\eta}_{0}- 
    \mathrlap{\hspace{0pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\eta}}}}}}\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\eta}}
_{0}\big)
    \boldsymbol{\upomega}\,.
\end{align*}
Moreover, we have $\boldsymbol{\chi}_{0}\boldsymbol{\eta}_{0}\boldsymbol{\upomega}=\mathbf{1}\neq0$ ---Example~\ref{ex: 1 is not 0}---.
Hence, by Lemma~\ref{lemma: univ prop Fock}, the subrepresentation $(\mathcal{SF}\oplus 
    \mathrlap{\hspace{2.5pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.89}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}}}}}\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}
)\boldsymbol{\upomega}$ generated by the cyclic action of the current modes on $\boldsymbol{\upomega}$ is isomorphic to $\mathscr{F}{\!\!\textnormal{\footnotesize\itshape ock}}$.
Since we have the inclusion $(\mathcal{SF}\oplus 
    \mathrlap{\hspace{2.5pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.89}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}}}}}\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}
)\boldsymbol{\upomega}\subset \mathcal{F}$, it only remains to prove $\mathcal{F} \subset (\mathcal{SF}\oplus 
    \mathrlap{\hspace{2.5pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.89}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}}}}}\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}
)\boldsymbol{\upomega}$.
Take any field $F\in\mathcal{F}$.
Since normal ordering is a surjective map ---Remark~\ref{rmk: norm ord surjective}---, we can find linear field polynomials $L_1,\ldots,L_n\in\mathcal{P}_{\textnormal{lin}}$ such that we have
\begin{align*}
    F
    \,=\,
    \, \rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \circ\,\circ$}}\,
    \big( L_1 + \textnormal{\small Null}\big)
    \cdots
    \big( L_n + \textnormal{\small Null}\big)
    \,\rotatebox[]{90}{\scalebox{.8}{$\ \circ\,\circ$}}\,\,.
\end{align*}
Since $L_i$ are linear, they must be a linear combination of the elements in the basis
\begin{align*}
	\big\{
	\boldsymbol{\chi}_{-k} \mathbf{1},

    \mathrlap{\hspace{0pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\chi}}}}}}\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\chi}}
_{-k} \mathbf{1},	\boldsymbol{\eta}_{-k} \mathbf{1},

    \mathrlap{\hspace{0pt}\overline{\scalebox{1}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\eta}}}}}}\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\eta}}
_{-k} \mathbf{1},
	\,\colon
	k\in{\mathbb Z}_{>0}
	\big\}
	\,\cup\,
	\big\{
	\boldsymbol{\uptheta},
	\boldsymbol{\upxi}
	\big\}\,.
\end{align*}
Finally, by Lemma~\ref{lemma: normal order of basis}, the multilinearlity of normal ordering, and the relations between ground states via the current modes ---Remark~\ref{rmk: action currents on ground states}---, the local field $F$ can be written as an element of the submodule $(\mathcal{SF}\oplus 
    \mathrlap{\hspace{2.5pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.89}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}}}}}\ensuremath{\mathcal{SF}}
)\boldsymbol{\upomega}$.

\label{lemma: converg diagonal greens fnctn}
Let $\mathbf{z}^\delta\in\Omega^{\delta}$ be the closest vertex to $z\in\Omega$ in $\Omega^{\delta}$.
We have
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{G}_{\Omega^{\delta}}(\mathbf{z}^\delta,\mathbf{z}^\delta)
    +
    \frac{1}{2\pi}\log \delta
    \mspace{20mu}
    \xrightarrow{\mspace{40mu}\delta \downarrow 0 \mspace{40mu}}
    \mspace{20mu}
    \mathbf{g}_{\Omega}(z,z)
    +
    \frac{\mathsf{C}}{2\pi}
    \,,
\end{align*}
uniformly for $z$ in compacts of $\Omega$, where $\mathbf{g}_{\Omega}$ is the harmonic part of the Green's function on the domain~$\Omega$ as in Section~\ref{subsec: corr fun symplectic fermions}.

\label{coro: converg of partner}
Let $\mathbf{z}^\delta\in\Omega^{\delta}$ be the closest vertex to $z\in\Omega$ in $\Omega^{\delta}$ and fix $\lambda > 0$.
We have
\begin{align*}
    -\Big\langle \xi(\mathbf{z}^\delta)\theta(\mathbf{z}^\delta) \Big\rangle_{\Omega^{\delta}}^{\textnormal{fDGFF}}
    - 2\, \log \big( \lambda \delta \big)
    \mspace{20mu}
    \xrightarrow{\mspace{40mu}\delta \downarrow 0 \mspace{40mu}}
    \mspace{20mu}
    \Big\langle \boldsymbol{\omega}(z) \Big\rangle_{\Omega; \alpha(\lambda)}^{\textnormal{SyFe}}
\end{align*}
uniformly for $z$ in compacts of $\textnormal{Conf}_{n}( \Omega )$, where $\alpha(\lambda)\coloneqq 2 \big( \log \lambda + \mathsf{C} \big)$.

By linearity of the correlation functions, we only need to prove the statement for fields $\boldsymbol{\varphi}_i$ in the basis~\eqref{eq: basis}.
For the sake of brevity, we detail the proof in the case where we have
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{\varphi}_i
\,\coloneqq\,
\boldsymbol{\chi}_{-k_{r_i}^{(i)}}\cdots
&
\boldsymbol{\chi}_{-k_1^{(i)}}
\boldsymbol{\eta}_{-\ell_{s_i}^{(i)}}\cdots\boldsymbol{\eta}_{-\ell_1^{(i)}}
\boldsymbol{\upomega}
\in \mathcal{F}\,
\end{align*}
with
${0 \leq k_1^{(i)} < k_2^{(i)} < \cdots < k_{r_i}^{(i)}}$
and
${0 \leq \ell_1^{(i)} < \ell_2^{(i)} < \cdots < \ell_{s_i}^{(i)}}$---the more general case can be \linebreak[4] handled in a similar way.

\noindent
The CFT correlation functions of the fields $\boldsymbol{\varphi}_1,\ldots,\boldsymbol{\varphi}_n\in\mathscr{F}{\!\!\textnormal{\footnotesize\itshape ock}}$ ($\cong\mathcal{F}$) can be expressed ---recall Remark~\ref{rmk: integral formula}--- as
\begin{align} \label{eq: CFT corr fun}
    &
    \Big\langle 
    \boldsymbol{\varphi}_1(z_1)
    \,\cdots\,
    \boldsymbol{\varphi}_n(z_n)
     \Big\rangle_{\Omega; \alpha}^{\textnormal{SyFe}}
    \,=\phantom{\Bigg\vert}
    \\ \nonumber
    &
    \mspace{60mu}
    =\,
    \oint \!\cdots\! \oint
    \prod_{i=1}^n
    \Bigg(
    \prod_{a=1}^{r_i}
    \frac{\textnormal{d} \zeta_{i;a}^{\eta}}{2\pi\mathbbm{i}}
    \prod_{b=1}^{s_i}
    \frac{\textnormal{d} \zeta_{i;b}^{\chi}}{2\pi\mathbbm{i}}
    \Bigg)
    \prod_{i=1}^n
    \Bigg(
    \prod_{a=1}^{r_i}
    \, \big( \zeta_{i;a}^{\eta} - z_i \big)^{-k_a^{(i)}}
    \prod_{b=1}^{s_i}
    \, \big( \zeta_{i;b}^{\chi} - z_i \big)^{-\ell_b^{(i)}}
    \Bigg)
    \times
    \\ \nonumber
    &
    \mspace{150mu}
    \phantom{\Bigg\vert}
    \times
    \bigg\langle
    \boldsymbol{\eta}\big( \zeta_{1;1}^{\eta} \big) \cdots \boldsymbol{\eta}\big( \zeta_{1;r_1}^{\eta} \big)
    \boldsymbol{\chi}\big( \zeta_{1;1}^{\chi} \big) \cdots \boldsymbol{\chi}\big( \zeta_{1;s_1}^{\chi} \big)\,
    \boldsymbol{\omega}(z_1)
    \;\cdots
    \\ \nonumber
    &
    \mspace{290mu}
    \cdots\;
    \boldsymbol{\eta}\big( \zeta_{n;1}^{\eta} \big) \cdots \boldsymbol{\eta}\big( \zeta_{n;r_n}^{\eta} \big)
    \boldsymbol{\chi}\big( \zeta_{n;1}^{\chi} \big) \cdots \boldsymbol{\chi}\big( \zeta_{n;s_n}^{\chi} \big)\,
    \boldsymbol{\omega}(z_n)
    \bigg\rangle_{\Omega;\alpha}^{\textnormal{SyFe}}\,,
\end{align}
where the variables $\zeta_{i;a}^{\eta}$ and $\zeta_{i;b}^{\chi}$ are integrated, respectively, along contours $\Gamma_{i;a}^{\eta}$ and $\Gamma_{i;b}^{\chi}$ in $\Omega$ that encircle the point $z_i$ and no other insertion point, and such that all contours are pairwise non-intersecting.
An example of this scenario is shown in Figure~\ref{fig: proof}.

\noindent
In what remains of this proof, we omit some details---we refer the reader to the proof of Theorem~7.2 in \cite{ABK-DGFF_local_fields}.
In particular, in there, the contours and their discrete approxi\-mations are deliberately chosen to be of square shape so that some technical aspects substantially simplify.

\noindent
To set up the scaling-limit convergence,
we let $\gamma_{i;a}^{\eta}(\delta)$ and $\gamma_{i;b}^{\chi}(\delta)$ be discrete corner contours\footnote{Naturally, one needs to impose certain requirements to these discrete contours so that in the limit $\delta\downarrow0$ one gets proper Riemann-sum approximations of continuum contour integrals.} in ${\mathbb Z}^2$ that are, respectively, close to the blown-up contours $\frac{1}{\delta}\big(\Gamma_{i;a}^{\eta}-z_i\big)$ and $\frac{1}{\delta}\big(\Gamma_{i;b}^{\chi}-z_i\big)$.
Then, for the field~$\boldsymbol{\varphi}_i\in\mathcal{F}$, consider the $\mathcal{P}$-representative
\begin{align*}
    &
    P_i^\delta \,\coloneqq\,
    \prod_{a=1}^{r_i}
    \Bigg(
    \sqint^{\sharp}_{\gamma^\eta_{i;a}(\delta)}
    \frac{\textnormal{d}^{\sharp} \mathbf{u}_{i;a}}{2\pi \mathbbm{i}}
    (\mathbf{u}_{i;a})_\diamond^{[-k_a^{(i)}]}
    \partial^\bullet_\sharp \boldsymbol\uptheta \big( (\mathbf{u}_{i;a})_\textnormal{m} \big)
    \Bigg)
    \times
    \\
    &
    \mspace{150mu}
    \times\,
    \prod_{b=1}^{s_i}
    \Bigg(
    \sqint^{\sharp}_{\gamma^\chi_{i;b}(\delta)} \,
    \frac{\textnormal{d}^{\sharp} \mathbf{v}_{i;b}}{2\pi \mathbbm{i}}
    (\mathbf{v}_{i;b})_\diamond^{[-\ell_b^{(i)}]}
    \partial^\bullet_\sharp \boldsymbol\upxi \big( (\mathbf{v}_{i;b})_\textnormal{m} \big)
    \Bigg)
    \Big( -\boldsymbol\upxi(0)\boldsymbol\uptheta(0) - 2\log\big(\lambda\delta\big)\Big)
\end{align*}
of the field~$\boldsymbol{\varphi}_i-\log(\lambda\delta)\big[({\mathbf{L}}_{0}- 
    \mathrlap{\hspace{-1pt}\overline{\scalebox{0.85}[1]{\phantom{\ensuremath{\mathbf{L}}}}}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{L}}
_{0})-(\Delta_i+\bar\Delta_i)\big]\boldsymbol{\varphi}_i$,
where we have used the basis expression in Example~\ref{ex: good basis scaling fields}, the representative~$\boldsymbol{\upomega} = -\boldsymbol\upxi(0)\boldsymbol\uptheta(0)+\textnormal{\small Null}$ ---Example~\ref{ex: rep of ground states}---, and the integral definition of the current modes ---Proposition~\ref{prop: current modes}---.
